  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity climb to Advisory level today and \nlingers through Friday, possibly into the weekend.\n\nA Bermuda high begins to build in across the western Atlantic Ocean \nand southerly flow continues today. Temperatures and dew points have \nalready begun to rise: T of 73 (up from 69 just before midnight) and \nTd of 71 (up from 67). They'll only continue to surge throughout the \nday and again on Friday, with temperatures likely topping out near \n90 and oppressive dew points near 75, leading to \"feels like\" \ntemperatures climbing into the mid to upper 90s... possibly even \nnear 100 at times. Greatest risk of heat indicies reaching triple \ndigits remains in the CT and Merrimack River Valleys where localized \ndownslope winds could help boost temperatures. Some uncertainty \nremains regarding the magnitude of heat elsewhere due to the chance \nfor increasing cloud cover from any thunderstorms that develop today \nand Friday afternoons. Expecting poor overnight relief heading into \nFriday with nighttime temperatures hovering 71-73 as well as \ndewpoints struggling to drop to 70... tonight's heat indicies are \nlikely to hover 77-80. The warm overnight temperatures will be \ndangerous for those without adequate access to cooling, especially \nin urban areas. \n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect from 11 AM today through 8 PM \nFriday, and future shifts may need to extend the Advisory into \nSaturday if the heat persists.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Diurnal pop-up thunderstorms remain a daily threat, \nbut are most likely Saturday, with a lower chance on Sunday. \nUnsettled weather may return early next week, as well as continuing \nhigh humidity.\n\nA few weak, upper level disturbances passing through New England \ncombining with the high heat/humidity and just enough instability \nwill likely be enough to pop a few diurnal showers/thunderstorms \neach day through the weekend, with greatest chances Saturday. While \nthe potential for storms to be severe is on the lower end, any \nthunderstorms that do pop have the potential to produce frequent \nlightning, gusty winds, and torrential downpours as PWATs will \nlikely be around 2.0\". Model soundings also indicate the mid-level \nsteering flow could be rather week, leading to slow moving storms or \neven training. Given the amount of rain southern New England has \nseen over the last week or so, any heavy downpours could lead to \nlocalized flooding.\n\nExpecting a few more shortwaves to move through the upper-level flow \nas well as a trailing cold front early next week, bringing \nadditional chances for showers to southern New England Monday night \ninto Tuesday. The FROPA could bring a small amount of relief from \nthe heat for the start of the next work week, but humidity levels \nlikely will remain elevated through mid-week.",
